      passage: "A. Beginning language learners often store vocabulary as pairs: one English word beside one translation. This is efficient when the goal is recognition. A learner can quickly understand that pressure means áp lực or that benefit means lợi ích. The weakness appears when the learner tries to produce a complete sentence. Knowing the meaning of a word does not automatically reveal which verbs, prepositions or grammatical patterns normally surround it.\n\nB. Fluent language use depends heavily on chunks. A chunk may be a fixed expression, such as on the other hand, or a flexible pattern, such as place pressure on someone. The individual words are not necessarily difficult. What matters is that they are frequently used together. When learners retrieve the whole pattern, they reduce the number of decisions required during speaking or writing.\n\nC. Literal translation can create combinations that are understandable but unusual. Vietnamese learners may produce make pollution, learn knowledge, discuss about, or the price is expensive. The listener can often guess the intended meaning, yet repeated unnatural combinations make communication less precise. They also slow the learner down because each sentence is being assembled from separate translated pieces.\n\nD. The solution is not to memorise thousands of expressions without context. A phrase becomes useful when the learner knows its meaning, pattern, register and possible variations. For example, raise awareness is common in neutral and academic writing. A learner should also notice related combinations such as public awareness, environmental awareness, and raise awareness of an issue. This network is easier to reuse than a single isolated translation.\n\nE. Personalisation adds another layer. After seeing a phrase in a model sentence, learners should create a sentence that is true for them. Be under pressure becomes more memorable when it appears in 'I was under pressure before my final exam.' The personal sentence may be simple, but it links the phrase to a real situation and gives the learner a ready example for Speaking.\n\nF. Repetition must also be spaced and varied. Using a phrase five times in one exercise may create short-term familiarity. Using it again the next day in Speaking, one week later in Writing and later in a different topic creates flexible knowledge. The aim is not to remember the page where the phrase appeared. It is to retrieve the phrase when the idea appears.\n\nG. Learners should still remain selective. Some expressions are common in essays but too formal for ordinary conversation. Others sound natural in Speaking but weak in academic Writing. Building a phrase bank therefore requires decisions about register. The best bank is not the largest one. It is the one whose contents the learner can use accurately, naturally and in the right place.",
