      passage: "A. Large goals are attractive because they create a clear picture of the future. A student may aim for a high examination score, an athlete may imagine finishing a marathon and a new employee may hope to become a manager. The difficulty is that distant outcomes provide little information about what to do on an ordinary Tuesday afternoon. When progress is slow, the goal can begin to feel more like a judgement than a guide.\n\nB. Researchers studying motivation have therefore paid increasing attention to small wins. A small win is not simply an easy task. It is a meaningful piece of progress that can be completed and recognised. Writing one clear paragraph, understanding one difficult concept or maintaining a routine for five days may appear modest, but each achievement provides evidence that effort is producing change.\n\nC. This evidence matters because motivation is not always the starting point of action. People often wait to feel motivated before beginning, yet motivation frequently grows after a task has started. Completing a manageable step reduces uncertainty and creates a sense of movement. The learner is no longer imagining progress. They can point to something that did not exist before.\n\nD. Small wins can also improve diagnosis. A broad goal such as 'improve Writing' hides many different problems. A narrower target, such as 'write topic sentences that give a clear position', can be tested. If the learner succeeds, the skill can be extended. If not, the problem becomes easier to identify. In this way, small tasks turn improvement into a series of experiments.\n\nE. However, small wins can be misused. Some people divide work into tiny actions that are easy to tick off but do not contribute meaningfully to the final goal. Colouring a timetable, downloading another application or copying twenty phrases may create activity without progress. A useful small win must be connected to a larger ability and leave behind a product, decision or change in performance.\n\nF. Rewards require similar care. A short break or enjoyable activity can help a learner maintain effort, but excessive external rewards may shift attention away from the value of the work itself. If a student studies only to receive a prize, motivation can disappear when the prize is removed. Recognition is most helpful when it draws attention to the process, such as improved consistency or a better repair strategy.\n\nG. The strongest learning systems combine long-term direction with short-term evidence. The major goal explains why the work matters. Small wins show whether the current method is moving the learner in that direction. Neither is sufficient alone. A large ambition without visible progress can become discouraging, while a collection of easy tasks without a larger purpose becomes empty productivity.",
